 "reviewed": "irregular past participles and missing essere auxiliaries",
 "_finding": "passato_prossimo is the second tense in TENSE_ORDER, so every learner reaches it early, and the engine built it from two data fields that were mostly empty. Only 60 of 1711 verbs carried a pastParticiple, so the regular -ato/-uto/-ito rule was inventing 'ho scrivuto', 'ho leggiuto', 'ho mettuto', 'ho nascuto'. And only 16 carried aux, so every verb of motion or change of state was given avere: 'ho partito', 'ho nato', 'ho caduto'. The very common verbs (fare, dire, stare, essere, avere, andare, venire, arrivare, rimanere) were already correct; the damage was all in the second tier. Verbs that genuinely take either auxiliary depending on transitivity --- correre, passare, cambiare, finire, cominciare, vivere, aumentare, diminuire --- are deliberately left on avere rather than asserting one reading.",
